<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Cambrai</h1><p class="st-meta">1007 CE – 1678 CE · capital Cambrai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An imperial church principality on the French border, which gave its name to the League of Cambrai and the Ladies' Peace before Louis XIV annexed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cambrai was a Merovingian bishopric whose diocese stretched across <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">Hainaut</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>. In 1007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry II</a> granted the county of Cambrai to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Erluin, making the bishops princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> on its frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Cambrésis was a small but rich land of cloth and linen, its name later given to the fine fabric cambric. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> often struggled with the townspeople and with the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">Hainaut</a>. The city hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1508 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cambrai">Ladies' Peace</a> of 1529. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> built a citadel there in 1543.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cambrai became an archbishopric in 1559 under strong Spanish influence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> captured it in 1677, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> of 1678 gave it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-fenelon">Fénelon</a> was its most famous archbishop.</p></div><aside class="st-side">